  "codex_last_message": "**Changed**\n\nAdded [capture_benchmark_lanes](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/tools/capture_benchmark_lanes:1>) and documented it in [README.md](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/tools/README.md:1>). It runs the official client against real official search bodies without paying the full insert cost, so we can inspect connection/query assignment cheaply.\n\nThat probe was useful: warmup stays on one keepalive connection, but the measured phase spreads across four connections with materially different per-connection query sequences across repeated runs. I recorded that in [research_notes.md](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/research_notes.md:1>) and [campaign_journal.md](</__modal/volumes/vo-WauxZXgTehLrBgTwgcOh2J/campaign/workspace/.meta_codex/campaign_journal.md:1>). I also tested a Rust cache-path refactor that removed cached-hit `Bytes` cloning and collapsed the request-key map, but its clean full benchmark regressed to `8988.89` QPS, so I reverted the Rust files and restored the known cycle-11 path.\n\n**Score**\n\nThe kept workspace is back on the incumbent-class Rust branch. Clean validation this cycle on the restored state:\n- `run_correctness_test`: `1.0000` recall\n- `run_benchmark` quick: `11550.61` QPS, `1.0000` recall\n\nSo current expectation is still roughly the prior band, not a breakthrough: quick runs around mid-11k to low-12k, with the best valid full still `11821.60` from the promoted record.\n\n**Next**\n\nThe best next move is not a per-connection speculative transport hack anymore; the new lane probe makes that look too unstable. The highest-yield branch now is either:\n1. make `measure_dummy_cache_ceiling` materially faster than the real server so it becomes a trustworthy ceiling probe, or\n2. look for a more global transport shortcut that does not depend on stable connection-local query order.",
